{"id": "EUVD-2026-103671", "enisaUuid": "0e6c8184-5958-3afc-bc52-b96f8c7309c6", "description": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nKVM: PPC: Book3S HV: fix secure device page leak on uv_page_in() failure\n\nIn kvmppc_svm_page_in(), if uv_page_in() fails after\nkvmppc_uvmem_get_page() has succeeded, the secure device page is never\nreleased.  kvmppc_uvmem_get_page() sets a bit in kvmppc_uvmem_bitmap,\nallocates a kvmppc_uvmem_page_pvt struct, marks the GFN as\nKVMPPC_GFN_UVMEM_PFN, and calls zone_device_page_init() which sets\nrefcount=1 and locks the page.  The subsequent goto out_finalize skips\nthe *mig.dst assignment, so migrate_vma_finalize() is a no-op for the\npage, and none of those resources are ever reclaimed.\n\nEach occurrence permanently consumes one entry from the firmware-bounded\nsecure memory pool (kvmppc_uvmem_bitmap), leaks pvt, and leaves the GFN\nmarked as secure \u2014 making it unusable for the lifetime of the VM.\n\nThe twin __kvmppc_svm_page_out() already handles the analogous uv_page_out()\nfailure correctly with unlock_page(dpage); __free_page(dpage).  Apply\nthe same pattern here: unlock_page() followed by put_page(), which\nchains through free_zone_device_folio() into kvmppc_uvmem_folio_free()\nto clear the bitmap bit, free pvt, and reset the GFN state.\n\nReachable whenever uv_page_in() returns an error (e.g. UV pool\nexhaustion) on any POWER9/10 + Ultravisor/PEF system.", "datePublished": "2026-10-18T14:38:18", "dateUpdated": "2026-10-19T09:19:29", "baseScore": 0.0, "references": ["https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/ed2f2ff63cb7e71204096e765c6d02c3b6974b95", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/8f3b7746874b39fc20e0363c9076c7787d4765ba", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/a933d7024359e2e566b07903d1294b84f90482ae", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/c41088b2765af1e97e7bb05f906ae1823eca0388", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/f3d4b2fc30c97cf8e6f8b880d2de9c17ecac7f5b", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/dfb84274932737e338ff5eb27f34b4c8ecb01b50", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/fcbff8008b0e08cd53b611bd0ff7e01f5b962f80", "https://git.kernel.org/stable/c/0a416ee20bcccddf91ca5b63696a23b9d11d73aa"], "aliases": ["CVE-2026-98433", "GHSA-3v64-xhg6-868f"], "assigner": "Linux", "epss": 0.16, "enisaIdProduct": [{"id": "06a0b724-e15c-3f11-b745-711f0b3bae1a", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "ca9f4942670c37407bb109090eaf776ce2ccc54c <a933d7024359e2e566b07903d1294b84f90482ae"}, {"id": "0e7722f5-49b4-399a-856f-f398aa6274a6", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 5.15.222"}, {"id": "18839b42-232d-3f55-a204-d6b99659cef2", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 5.10.271"}, {"id": "1e3b32da-a286-3933-bddb-61eb37f522d7", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.1.189"}, {"id": "3594b8f5-63e7-3b57-b8c1-0297b9576b22", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "5.5"}, {"id": "409a82aa-6095-3f3b-8728-7eaa6ba623f2", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 0"}, {"id": "42527018-2a68-3e1f-8ff5-c5f1e5dd53c8", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "ca9f4942670c37407bb109090eaf776ce2ccc54c <f3d4b2fc30c97cf8e6f8b880d2de9c17ecac7f5b"}, {"id": "4c9f7e2a-0699-3db1-a84d-7b5fac7aca38", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "ca9f4942670c37407bb109090eaf776ce2ccc54c <c41088b2765af1e97e7bb05f906ae1823eca0388"}, {"id": "5dfd6924-ceae-386c-a85e-e09602079167", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "patch: 6.12.112"}, {"id": "6533a84d-333c-33f7-aa39-32f7898c0a6c", "product": {"name": "Linux", "vendor": {"name": "Linux"}}, "product_version": "ca9f4942670c37407bb109090eaf776ce2ccc54c <ed2f2ff63cb7e71204096e765c6d02c3b6974b95"}, {"id": "91689